{
  "_comment": [
    "Treasurer of the United States and Secretary of the Treasury, as they",
    "appear on small-size US currency.",
    "",
    "Signatures are the differentiator between Friedberg variants of the same",
    "denomination and series year, so they are the field a lookup service most",
    "needs. They are also safe to seed in a way a Friedberg table is not: who",
    "held an office and when is historical fact, whereas the Friedberg",
    "numbering is a copyrighted arrangement.",
    "",
    "term_from and term_to bracket the years the pair signed together, so a",
    "picker can offer only the combinations possible for a note's series year",
    "instead of all of them. That is the intended use -- 'signature choices",
    "based on series'.",
    "",
    "Complete for Series 1928 through 2021, in order of use, as of 2026-09-16.",
    "The first eleven pairs were confirmed on 2026-09-07; the rest were added",
    "with note_issue.json, whose every row names two independent sources",
    "(Wikipedia's series tables, USPaperMoney's chronology, and BEP's serial",
    "table for Series 1996 on, which agrees throughout). An earlier attempt at",
    "a complete chronology returned corrupt rows and was discarded; this one",
    "was checked row by row against officers' terms. Two pairs outlast a term",
    "on real notes: Woods/Woodin on the $1 1928 United States Note (printed in",
    "1933) and Granahan/Barr on the $1 1963B.",
    "",
    "A pair added by hand is marked `manual`, and a later reload never",
    "overwrites it. Check it against the note itself before trusting it for a",
    "catalogue lookup.",
    "",
    "Naming: `code` is treasurer_secretary in lower case, which is how dealers",
    "write it (Julian/Morgenthau). `label` is the same pair as displayed."
  ],

